🔐 Lab: Username Enumeration via Different Responses
📖 What This Lab Is

This lab demonstrates a common authentication vulnerability called username enumeration. Username enumeration occurs
when a web application reveals whether a username exists in the system through different responses during login attempts.
In this lab, the login page responds differently depending on whether the username is valid or not. If an attacker
submits an invalid username, the application returns a message such as “Invalid username.” However, if the username
exists but the password is incorrect, the application returns “Incorrect password.”
These different responses allow attackers to determine which usernames are valid. Once a valid username is discovered,
an attacker can perform a password brute-force attack to gain unauthorized access to the account.
The objective of this lab is to identify a valid username and then brute-force the password to successfully log in to the 
user account.


⚙️ How This Vulnerability Works
The vulnerability occurs because the application leaks information through different error messages.
The process works like this:
The attacker sends login requests using different usernames.
The server responds differently depending on whether the username exists.
Invalid usernames produce the response “Invalid username.”
Valid usernames produce the response “Incorrect password.”
By comparing responses, attackers can identify which username exists in the system.
After finding a valid username, attackers try multiple passwords until the correct one is discovered.


🛠️ How to Solve the Lab

Step 1 — Capture Login Request
Open the lab login page.
Enter an invalid username and password.
Capture the request using Burp Suite Proxy.

Step 2 — Send Request to Intruder
Go to Proxy → HTTP History.
Find the POST /login request.
Send it to Burp Intruder.

Step 3 — Enumerate Valid Username
Highlight the username parameter.
Set it as the payload position.
Example:
username=§test-user§&password=test
Select Sniper attack type.
Use the candidate usernames wordlist as payload.
Start the attack.
After the attack finishes:
Look at the Length column in results.
One response will be different because it returns “Incorrect password.”
That username is valid.

Step 4 — Brute-Force the Password
Replace the username with the identified valid username.
Set payload position on the password parameter.
Example:
username=valid-user&password=§test-password§
Load the candidate password wordlist.
Start the attack.
Check the results:
Most responses return 200 status code.
One response returns 302 redirect.
This indicates a successful login.

Step 5 — Log In
Use the identified:
Valid Username
Correct Password
Log in to access the user account page and solve the lab.

🐍 Where Python Scripts Can Be Used
Python scripts can automate parts of this attack instead of using Burp Intruder.
They can be used for:

1️⃣ Username Enumeration
A Python script can send login requests using a username wordlist and check the response message or length to identify valid usernames.

2️⃣ Password Brute Force
After finding a valid username, another Python script can try multiple passwords from a password wordlist until a successful login response is detected.

3️⃣ Response Analysis

Python can automatically analyze:
response length
error messages
HTTP status codes
This helps identify anomalies that indicate valid credentials.
Libraries commonly used include:
requests
threading
wordlists
